</table></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g stroke-width="0.8pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M -195.87 -204.72 L 48.74 -204.72"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.64pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 -195.87 -204.72)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.16 2.88 C -1.98 1.8 0 0.18 0.54 0 C 0 -0.18 -1.98 -1.8 -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.64pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 48.74 -204.72)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.16 2.88 C -1.98 1.8 0 0.18 0.54 0 C 0 -0.18 -1.98 -1.8 -2.16 -2.88"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -132.46 -218.08)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.2em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:9.25pt;" height="8.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.65)" width="117.79"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.pic1.22" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Short total task time</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F2.sf2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">speculative step too large (<math id="S3.F2.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=6</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Limitations of fix speculative step approaches. A small <math id="S3.F2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> limits acceleration although it avoids waste, while a large <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> increases speedup at the cost of redundant computation when mismatches occur (step 5 &amp; 6 is wasted in (b)).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Speculative Planning Cost Broken-Down</span>  
While speculative execution offers the promise of improving latency by parallelizing candidate task executions in a lossless manner, it inevitably introduces additional token consumption. As shown in <a href="#S3.F2" title="In 3 Why is Speculative Planning Expensive? ‣ Dynamic Speculative Agent Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> (b), this overhead becomes particularly significant when <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is large, motivating the need for a more adaptive planning approach. In this section, we aim to understand where the major overheads arise by systematically breaking down the costs of speculative planning under different fixed-<math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> settings.
Redundant costs in speculative planning arise when token consumption does not contribute to the final generated plan or acceleration, which can be divided into four main components: redundant (1) the prompt tokens and (2) the generation tokens consumed by <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (3) the prompt tokens and (4) the generation tokens consumed by <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3 Why is Speculative Planning Expensive? ‣ Dynamic Speculative Agent Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> provides a detailed breakdown of token consumption for each component, comparing normal planning against fixed-<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> speculative planning across different <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>s with 2 speculative planning configurations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Approximation agent</span> incurs excessive wasted token generation when the speculative step <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the number of correct steps the agent can generate sequentially. When a mismatch occurs before reaching <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, all steps that have already been generated based on the invalidated step become wasted.
This effect intensifies under large-<math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> configurations, where the system frequently issues more speculative steps, only to discard them upon early divergence. In experiments where we adopt LLM with direct generation as <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and LLM with chain-of-thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., (2022)</a></cite> as <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s waste primarily stems from prompt tokens rather than generation tokens. This occurs because both agents employ relatively simple planning strategies with moderate generation length while contain lengthy prompts including environment introduction, task descriptions, and past action trajectories.
Consequently, speculative executions of <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> incur significantly higher prompt costs than generation costs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.01920v1/openagi_react_cost_comp.svg" id="S3.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Direct-ReAct</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.01920v1/openagi_mad_cost_comp.svg" id="S3.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/286;" width="476" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CoT-MAD</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Token cost breakdown under speculative planning for different fixed <math id="S3.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values on the OpenAGI benchmark, shown for two distinct <math id="S3.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> configurations: (a) Direct (<math id="S3.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) with ReAct (<math id="S3.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>), and (b) CoT (<math id="S3.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) with Multi-Agent Debate (<math id="S3.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>). Each bar contains four segments: <math id="S3.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s prompt (A-P) and generation (A-G) tokens, and <math id="S3.F3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s prompt (T-P) and generation (T-G) tokens. These components are also aggregated into total prompt (Total-P)and generation tokens (Total-G), and overall total tokens (Total). In both subfigures, higher values of <math id="S3.F3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> result in substantially greater total token costs.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Target agent</span> also incurs excessive wasted token generation from mismatches between proposed actions from <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These mismatches introduce two additional sources of redundant costs: (1) <span id="S3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Completed <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> steps</span>: When a mismatch occurs at a particular step, all subsequent completed target steps become invalid, rendering their prompt and generation tokens entirely wasted. (2) <span id="S3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ongoing target steps</span>: All subsequent in-progress target steps after a mismatch are immediately canceled. These steps may have already initiated LLM calls, consumed prompt tokens, and generated partial outputs before forced cancellation. This type of waste grows proportionally with <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> because Speculative Planning concurrently launches <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step. Larger <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> naturally lead to more target steps running ahead of validation, amplifying the potential waste caused by each mismatch.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variation of Optimal <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span>   An optimal <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the number of speculative steps from the current state up to the first mismatch, achieving maximal speedup without generating any invalid tokens. The analysis of 312 tasks from the OpenAGI benchmark reveals substantial variability in the optimal speculation step <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> even within the same task. For each task, we computed the maximum and minimum optimal <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> across different execution trajectories, as well as the variance and the difference (max–min) of these values: on average across tasks, the maximum optimal <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> was 3.53 and the minimum was 1.60, with a mean variance of 1.46 and an average range of 1.93. The observed intra-task variability in optimal <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> suggests that a fixed setting is inadequate for diverse scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implications for Dynamic Speculative Planning</span>   The cost analysis and heterogeneity of optimal <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> reveal an inherent tension in speculation step selection: the optimal <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> varies significantly across different planning stages within a single task and thereby <span id="S3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">small <math id="S3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> limits acceleration by under-utilizing available concurrency, while <span id="S3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">large <math id="S3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math></span> frequently trigger excessive token generation which increase costs. Due to the heterogeneous difficulty of step proposals within tasks, no single optimal <math id="S3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> exists throughout the plan.
Therefore by adaptively modulating <math id="S3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> based on contextual factors, such as task characteristics, step complexity, and prediction confidence, a dynamic approach should strategically balance acceleration and efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Beyond mere adaptation to task characteristics, a dynamic approach also creates an opportunity for user-controlled efficiency trade-offs.</span> As model pricing structures and organizational priorities evolve, the optimal balance between latency reduction and cost efficiency varies considerably across deployment contexts. Some applications may prioritize near-instantaneous responses regardless of cost (<em id="S3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, critical decision support) or operate in environments where prompt tokens are inexpensive, thus minimizing the financial impact of additional token generation. Others may emphasize cost efficiency with moderate acceleration (<em id="S3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, batch processing systems) or operate under constraints where excessive generation tokens or high-priced prompt tokens significantly impact operational budgets. By exposing this latency-cost continuum through configurable parameters, dynamic speculative planning empowers practitioners to precisely calibrate the system.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Dynamic Speculative Planning with User Controllability</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dynamic Speculative Planning begins with a simple intuition: once <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> has produced a few actions, it is often possible to forecast how many additional actions it will generate correctly before <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> must intervene. Rather than selecting <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> heuristically or relying on an expensive offline model, we learn to predict it <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">online</em> while the system is running. After one speculative execution breaks either due to a detected mismatch or when a previously the step is exhausted, the system logs a training pair (partial trajectory, <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>) for training while at the same time utilize the latest checkpoint of the predictor to predict the next <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. These training pairs are collected to train a small DistilBERT-based regressor whose training cost can be negligible compared with inference cost of a single LLM call. To avoid the training time impacting the speed of the system, the predictor training is done asynchronously: for every newly collected pairs of datapoints, an independent thread fine<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>tunes the predictor to convergence and then writes the updated checkpoint to disk. The orchestration layer always utilizes the most recent checkpoint, so learning progresses without ever blocking agent planning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The online, multi-threaded training regimen offers two key advantages: (1) <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Real<span id="S4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>time adaptation</span>: the predictor is continuously updated with the most recent user interactions, keeping it aligned with the current usage distribution. (2) <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompt cost savings</span>: the system begins reducing expenses as soon as new data arrive, without waiting to accumulate and curate a large offline dataset.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Speculative<span id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>Step Control as Value Predictions</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As mentioned above, achieving dynamic speculative planning requires predicting the <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for each (partial) trajectory.
Moreover, this predictor must be updated online to enable continuous adaptation.
These requirements make online reinforcement learning a suitable framework.
Specifically, we can formalize speculative step prediction as a state-value prediction problem in reinforcement learning and apply Temporal-Difference (TD) learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton and Barto, 2018</a>)</cite> to update a state-value function in an online and incremental manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of all probability distributions supported over the set <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider a Markov decision process (MDP), <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=({\mathcal{S}},{\mathcal{A}},\mathrm{P},p_{0},R,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=({\mathcal{S}},{\mathcal{A}},\mathrm{P},p_{0},R,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, where the state space <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of token sequences, the action space <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of speculative steps, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{P}:{\mathcal{S}}\times{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\Delta({\mathcal{S}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{P}:{\mathcal{S}}\times{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\Delta({\mathcal{S}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the transition function, the initial state distribution <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability distribution over the prompt dataset <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}\in\Delta(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}\in\Delta(Q)</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:{\mathcal{S}}\times{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:{\mathcal{S}}\times{\mathcal{A}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function, and <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">An approximation agent <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> interacts with the MDP based on a policy <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathcal{S}}\rightarrow\Delta({\mathcal{A}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathcal{S}}\rightarrow\Delta({\mathcal{A}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for each episode, the agent starts from a prompt <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{0}\sim p_{0}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{0}\sim p_{0}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
At each time-step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, it observes the state <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}\in{\mathcal{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}\in{\mathcal{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, takes an action (<em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, a speculative step) <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="a_{t}\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}\sim\pi(\cdot|s_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, transitions to a new state <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and receives a scalar reward <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t+1}=R(s_{t},a_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t+1}=R(s_{t},a_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The episode ends when an approximation-target mismatch is met.
In our case, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply a concatenation of the token sequences of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t+1}=s_{t}|a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t+1}=s_{t}|a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also set <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
After an episode ends, we get a complete trajectory and it is formally defined as <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=(s_{0},a_{0},r_{1},s_{1},\dots,s_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=(s_{0},a_{0},r_{1},s_{1},\dots,s_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the final time-step.
Define return <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> as the total (discounted) reward from time-step <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}=\sum_{i=t}^{T-1}\gamma^{i-t}R(s_{i},a_{i})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}=\sum_{i=t}^{T-1}\gamma^{i-t}R(s_{i},a_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math>
A state-value function is defined as the expected return under policy <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[G_{t}|s_{t}=s]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{\pi}[G_{t}|s_{t}=s].</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The reward function is designed so that the return <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the speculative step at time-step <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Specifically, if the speculative step <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (generated by <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>) differs from the one generated by <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(s_{t},a_{t})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(s_{t},a_{t})=1</annotation></semantics></math>; otherwise, <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> gets zero reward and the episode ends immediately.
Under this reward setting, the state-value <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\pi}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\pi}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> is essentially the expected speculative step of state <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> is usually approximated by a neural network <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the network weights.
To learn a good approximation of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply TD learning and minimize the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L_{\theta}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}[(G_{t}^{\lambda}-V_{\theta}(s_{t}))^{2}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L_{\theta}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}[(G_{t}^{\lambda}-V_{\theta}(s_{t}))^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> is known as <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-return <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton and Barto, 2018</a>)</cite>.
Formally, it is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle G_{t}^{\lambda}=(1-\lambda)\sum_{n=1}^{T-t-1}\lambda^{n-1}G_{t:t+n}+\lambda^{T-t-1}G_{t}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{t}^{\lambda}=(1-\lambda)\sum_{n=1}^{T-t-1}\lambda^{n-1}G_{t:t+n}+\lambda^{T-t-1}G_{t},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="G_{t:t+n}=r_{t+1}+\gamma r_{t+2}+\cdots+\gamma^{n-1}r_{t+n}+\gamma^{n}V_{\theta}(s_{t+n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t:t+n}=r_{t+1}+\gamma r_{t+2}+\cdots+\gamma^{n-1}r_{t+n}+\gamma^{n}V_{\theta}(s_{t+n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The usage of <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-return offers a flexible way to achieve a better bias-variance tradeoff by interpolating between Monte Carlo return <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (when <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>) and 1-step TD target <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t+1}+\gamma V_{\theta}(s_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t+1}+\gamma V_{\theta}(s_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> (when <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0</annotation></semantics></math>).
It is worth noting that when <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, TD learning in this context reduces to online supervised learning since <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}^{1}=G_{t}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}^{1}=G_{t}=k</annotation></semantics></math>, thus our learning framework is a more generalized framework.
During training, since rewards depend on the speculative steps generated by <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> runs slower than <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we wait until <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> finishes and then compute rewards.
However, during deployment, a trained state-value function <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to predict the speculative step immediately, without waiting for <math id="S4.SS1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> to finish.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Multi-Thread Online Learning</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To minimize inference latency and update overhead, our system adopts a fully asynchronous learning framework that decouples predictor inference and training from the core execution pipeline: (1) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">asynchronous inference for parallel execution</span>: To mitigate the impact of predictor latency on system throughput, we perform speculative step prediction asynchronously at the start of each episode. The predictor runs concurrently with <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s planning step, and typically completes before <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s current step finishes. This parallelism effectively hides prediction latency, ensuring uninterrupted speculative planning.
(2) <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">asynchronous training for continuous adaptation</span>: To adapt to evolving task distributions, the predictor is updated online via a decoupled training-inference architecture. We maintain two models: a <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">training</em> predictor that undergoes continuous updates, and an <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inference</em> predictor that serves all runtime inference requests.
Upon completion of each speculative episode, an asynchronous thread is launched to train the predictor on the collected training dataset, which operates independently of the core planning pipeline. Once the training finishes, updated weights are immediately synchronized from the training predictor to the in-memory inference predictor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Controlling the Cost-Latency Trade-Off</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In speculative planning, the balance between cost and latency is intrinsically tied to the predicted step <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, by adjusting the estimation bias of the step <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we allow users to control the trade-off between cost and latency according to their specific needs.
There are in general two approaches for adjusting the speculative step <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>: one implemented during predictor training through modified loss functions, and another applied at inference time through post-prediction adjustment parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Biased <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Prediction</span>   One method to flexibly modulate the estimation bias of step values is to implement expectile regression <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostrikov et al., 2022</a>)</cite> during training. This approach applies asymmetric penalties that systematically shift the predicted values in a desired direction, allowing for principled control over the bias-variance tradeoff in step prediction.
Essentially, expectile regression generalizes ordinary least square (OLS) regression by replacing (symmetric) mean squared error (MSE) with an asymmetric MSE: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim{\mathcal{D}}}[L^{\tau}_{2}(y-f(x))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim{\mathcal{D}}}[L^{\tau}_{2}(y-f(x))]</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\tau}_{2}(u)=|\tau-\mathbf{1}(u&lt;0)|u^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\tau}_{2}(u)=|\tau-\mathbf{1}(u&lt;0)|u^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> determines which expectile to estimate.
The loss reduces to MSE when <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In DSP, we apply expectile regression to train the step predictor <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}(s)</annotation></semantics></math>, by simply replacing MSE in <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4.1 Speculative-Step Control as Value Predictions ‣ 4 Dynamic Speculative Planning with User Controllability ‣ Dynamic Speculative Agent Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> with the asymmetric MSE: <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\theta}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}[L^{\tau}_{2}(G_{t}^{\lambda}-V_{\theta}(s_{t}))]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\theta}=\mathbb{E}_{\tau\sim\pi}[L^{\tau}_{2}(G_{t}^{\lambda}-V_{\theta}(s_{t}))].</annotation></semantics></math>
By tuning the value of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, we can flexibly balance cost and latency in dynamic speculative planning.
When <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, a negative prediction error (<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}^{\lambda}-V_{\theta}(s_{t})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}^{\lambda}-V_{\theta}(s_{t})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) incurs a lower penalty, causing <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> to overestimate <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, which leads to faster execution but higher cost.
Conversely, when <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&lt;0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&lt;0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\theta}(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\theta}(s)</annotation></semantics></math> tends to underestimate <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}^{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>λ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}^{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in lower cost but increased execution latency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> with Biased Offset</span>   Another way to flexibly control the bias is to directly add or minus some offset to the unbiased predicted <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> without retraining the predictor. This method offers a straightforward and convenient mechanism for implementing user-controlled preferences in the latency-cost tradeoff. After the predictor generates its initial unbiased estimate <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we apply a user-specified bias parameter <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in{\mathbb{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in{\mathbb{N}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the final step value: <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=\max(1,\hat{k}+\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=\max(1,\hat{k}+\beta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Positive values of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> shift predictions toward more aggressive speculation while negative values produce more conservative predictions. The maximum function ensures that the final step is at least 1, maintaining a valid speculation step.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Main Experiment</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section describes the experimental setup and evaluation results. We assess our approach on two benchmarks: OpenAGI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2024</a>)</cite> which focuses on complex decision-making tasks that require reasoning over both vision and language modalities including 312 multi-step tasks, and TravelPlanner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2024</a>)</cite> which targets travel planning scenarios with 180 tasks. We evaluate the performance of our dynamic speculative planning framework and compare it to a set of baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The speculative step predictor</span> uses DistilBERT for its efficiency and expressive capacity in forecasting the speculation step <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> from a given state. The model is fine-tuned using expectile regression to learn the bias and adaptively adjust its predictions based on the collected data. The model is optimized using AdamW with a learning rate of <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math>. Each training process iterates over <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> epochs on a randomly sampled replay buffer (size <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2,\!500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2,\!500</annotation></semantics></math>). For multi-step return estimation, we adopt <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-return blending (<math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>) to balance bias-variance trade-offs during reward propagation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM backbones for agents</span> from the GPT and DeepSeek families are used in DSP. Below are their cost profiles:
(1) <em id="S5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT-4.1-mini</em> costs $0.40/$1.60 (prompt/generation per million tokens).
(2) <em id="S5.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">DeepSeek</em>: We use DeepSeek-chat as <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s backbone and DeepSeek-reasoner as <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>’s backbone, with respective costs of $0.27/$1.10 and $0.55/$2.19.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines</span> are chosen to be speculative planning with fixed <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, which is chosen independently of the environment’s characteristics or the task requirements.
We evaluate three configurations for the baseline with <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{2,4,6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{2,4,6\}</annotation></semantics></math> which are incrementally more aggressive in efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Four DSP configurations</span> are utilized for experiments: (1) <em id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Direct-Generation</em>: <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> generates the action directly for each step in a single LLM call, without any intermediate reasoning. (2) <em id="S5.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Chain-of-Thought (CoT)</em>: <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> first reasons about the current step and then generates the action based on the reasoning, all within a single LLM call. (3) <em id="S5.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ReAct</em>: <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> first deliberates on the action to take, then generates the action through two distinct LLM calls. (4) <em id="S5.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multi-agent Debate (MAD)</em>: two target agents debate in two rounds, with each agent proposing and refining actions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluation metrics are designed along three critical dimensions to assess speculative planning (SP) systems: <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">latency reduction</em>, <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">resource overhead</em>, and <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concurrency dynamics</em>. This triad captures the trade-offs in real-world deployment, where effective systems must accelerate task completion while respecting resource constraints and API concurrency limits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Efficiency and economy metrics are reported through two complementary perspectives:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Relative change metrics (<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> quantify performance against sequential execution, capturing <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">net improvements or overheads</span> introduced by speculative planning.
<span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute ratio metrics (<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> measure resource consumption relative to the fixed <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> baseline, enabling <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cross-strategy comparison</span> for practical deployment decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The fixed <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> baseline is adopted as it represents the minimal non-trivial setting that offers meaningful acceleration with minimal resource cost. As empirically validated on the OpenAGI benchmark, this configuration typically provides 15%-25% latency reduction (<math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\text{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>T</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\text{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) compared to sequential planning while maintaining low token overhead. This makes it an ideal reference point for comparing more advanced speculation strategies. Specifically:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{T}(\times)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>T</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{T}(\times)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{P}(\times)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>P</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{P}(\times)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{G}(\times)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>G</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{G}(\times)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cost}(\times)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Cost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cost}(\times)</annotation></semantics></math> denote absolute ratios of latency, prompt tokens, generation tokens, and monetary cost versus the <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> baseline.
<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\text{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>T</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\text{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\text{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>P</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\text{P}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\text{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>G</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\text{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\text{Cost}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Cost</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\text{Cost}</annotation></semantics></math> represent relative percentage changes versus sequential planning.
<math id="S5.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{MC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{MC}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes average peak concurrency (maximum parallel LLM calls), and <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{K}</annotation></semantics></math> is the average predicted step length per episode.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Time Decrease (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>T</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Quantifies the relative latency reduction when using speculative planning.
Specifically, for each task, we calculate the total time taken to complete the task under SP (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\text{SP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\text{SP}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and sequential planning (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\text{seq}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\text{seq}}</annotation></semantics></math>). The percentage time saving is then calculated as:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\text{Time}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(1-\frac{T^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{T^{\text{seq}}_{i}}\right)\times 100\%" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Time</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\text{Time}}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(1-\frac{T^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{T^{\text{seq}}_{i}}\right)\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of tasks. Positive values indicate time savings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. Cost and Token Increase</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Token increase captures the <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">redundant</em> resources consumed by speculative planning as a result of incorrect speculation. While the primary goal of speculative planning is to reduce latency and enhance efficiency, it still incurs a minimum cost. Even with perfect accuracy in speculative planning (<em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, the approximation agent makes correct predictions for every step), the cost must at least include the <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">baseline cost</em> — the minimal overhead associated with sequential planning of both the target and approximation agents. Thus, we define the baseline for token increase as the sum of the planning costs for the target agent and approximation agent operating sequentially, which represents the least amount of resources required for completing the task with speculation. Any increase in token usage beyond this baseline reflects the additional overhead introduced by speculative failures, capturing the waste generated from incorrect predictions by the approximation agent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the cost per token can differ for <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prompt tokens</em> and <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generation tokens</em>, we separately evaluate these components and also provide a combined metric for <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total cost increase</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Token Increase (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> This measures the increase in prompt tokens due to redundant prompt usage in SP. Let <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{seq}}_{i}=p^{\text{target}}_{i}+p^{\text{approx}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>approx</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{seq}}_{i}=p^{\text{target}}_{i}+p^{\text{approx}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the baseline prompt tokens (the sum of target and approximation prompts under sequential execution), and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the prompt tokens used under SP (parallel execution). The prompt token increase is calculated as:</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta P=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{p^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{p^{\text{seq}}_{i}}-1\right)\times 100\%" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta P=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{p^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{p^{\text{seq}}_{i}}-1\right)\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generation Token Increase (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta G</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> This metric captures the increase in generation tokens caused by incorrect speculation. Let <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\text{seq}}_{i}=g^{\text{target}}_{i}+g^{\text{approx}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>approx</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\text{seq}}_{i}=g^{\text{target}}_{i}+g^{\text{approx}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the baseline generation tokens (the sum of target and approximation generations under sequential execution), and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> the generation tokens used under SP. The generation token increase is calculated as:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta G=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{g^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{g^{\text{seq}}_{i}}-1\right)\times 100\%" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta G=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{g^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{g^{\text{seq}}_{i}}-1\right)\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Total Cost Increase (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{\text{Cost}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Cost</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{\text{Cost}}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> This metric quantifies the relative increase in cost between speculative planning (SP) and sequential planning (seq) strategies. Specifically, we define the total cost increase as:</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\text{Cost}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{PC^{\text{SP}}_{i}+GC^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{PC^{\text{seq}}_{i}+GC^{\text{seq}}_{i}}-1\right)\times 100\%" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Cost</mtext></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\text{Cost}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\left(\frac{PC^{\text{SP}}_{i}+GC^{\text{SP}}_{i}}{PC^{\text{seq}}_{i}+GC^{\text{seq}}_{i}}-1\right)\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PC^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PC^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PC^{\text{seq}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PC^{\text{seq}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the prompt costs of task <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> for SP and sequential strategies, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GC^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GC^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GC^{\text{seq}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GC^{\text{seq}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the corresponding generation costs. These costs are computed as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PC^{\text{SP}}_{i}=ap^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ap}}+tp^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tp}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>ap</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>tp</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PC^{\text{SP}}_{i}=ap^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ap}}+tp^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tp}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PC^{\text{seq}}_{i}=ap^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ap}}+tp^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tp}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>ap</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>tp</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PC^{\text{seq}}_{i}=ap^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ap}}+tp^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tp}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GC^{\text{SP}}_{i}=ag^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ag}}+tg^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tg}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>ag</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>tg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GC^{\text{SP}}_{i}=ag^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ag}}+tg^{\text{SP}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GC^{\text{seq}}_{i}=ag^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ag}}+tg^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tg}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>ag</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>tg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GC^{\text{seq}}_{i}=ag^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{ag}}+tg^{\text{seq}}_{i}\cdot\text{cost}_{\text{tg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ap^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ap^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tp^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tp^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the number of prompt tokens for the approximation and target in the SP strategy at task <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ap^{\text{seq}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ap^{\text{seq}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tp^{\text{seq}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tp^{\text{seq}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the number of prompt tokens for the approximation and target in the sequential planning at task <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ag^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ag^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tg^{\text{SP}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>SP</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tg^{\text{SP}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ag^{\text{seq}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ag^{\text{seq}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tg^{\text{seq}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>seq</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tg^{\text{seq}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the corresponding number of generation tokens. For cost, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cost}_{\text{ap}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>ap</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cost}_{\text{ap}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cost}_{\text{tp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>tp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cost}_{\text{tp}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the costs for the approximation and target API for prompt tokens, while <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cost}_{\text{ag}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>ag</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cost}_{\text{ag}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{cost}_{\text{tg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>cost</mtext><mtext>tg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{cost}_{\text{tg}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the corresponding costs for generation tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">By computing the total cost increase, we can quantify the trade-off between speculative planning and sequential planning in terms of the associated computational costs, helping to assess the efficiency of the proposed approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">3. Absolute Time Ratio (T(<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>))</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This metric quantifies the ratio of the absolute time in the current configuration compared to the baseline configuration (fix <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>), as it represents the minimal non-trivial setting that offers meaningful acceleration with minimal resource cost. It is calculated as:</p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{T}(\times)=\frac{T^{\text{current}}}{T^{\text{k=2}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>T</mi><mtext>current</mtext></msup><msup><mi>T</mi><mtext>k=2</mtext></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{T}(\times)=\frac{T^{\text{current}}}{T^{\text{k=2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">4. Absolute Cost and Token Ratio</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This metric measures the ratio of the absolute token usage and cost in the current configuration compared to the baseline configuration (fix <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute Prompt Token Ratio (P(<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>))</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{P}(\times)=\frac{p^{\text{current}}_{i}}{p^{\text{k=2}}_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>current</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>k=2</mtext></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{P}(\times)=\frac{p^{\text{current}}_{i}}{p^{\text{k=2}}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute Generation Token Ratio (G(<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>))</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{G}(\times)=\frac{g^{\text{current}}_{i}}{g^{\text{k=2}}_{i}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>G</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>current</mtext></msubsup><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext>k=2</mtext></msubsup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{G}(\times)=\frac{g^{\text{current}}_{i}}{g^{\text{k=2}}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Absolute Cost Ratio (Cost(<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>))</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Cost}(\times)=\frac{C^{\text{current}}}{C^{\text{k=2}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Cost</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">×</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>C</mi><mtext>current</mtext></msup><msup><mi>C</mi><mtext>k=2</mtext></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Cost}(\times)=\frac{C^{\text{current}}}{C^{\text{k=2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">5. Concurrency Dynamics</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluating the concurrency behavior of speculative planning (SP) is essential for understanding its real-world deployability. Modern systems often impose limits on simultaneous API requests due to infrastructure constraints or cost-efficiency considerations. Excessive concurrency can lead to API throttling, degraded performance, or instability, making it critical to monitor and control parallel request patterns. To quantify this behavior, we introduce two metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Max Concurrent API Calls (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{MC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{MC}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> For each task <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="MC_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">MC_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the peak number of overlapping API requests during speculative execution. <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{MC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{MC}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as:</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{MC}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}{MC}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{MC}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}{MC}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of tasks. This metric reflects how aggressively a system exploits parallelism and whether it respects concurrency constraints under varying speculative policies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Average Speculative Step (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{K}</annotation></semantics></math>)</span> In addition to peak concurrency (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{MC}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{MC}</annotation></semantics></math>), which captures brief spikes of load, we aim to characterize the <em id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">average</em> concurrency of the planning process over the planning process, as it correlates with sustained system pressure. However, the instantaneous concurrency varies continuously over time. Precisely computing the time-averaged number of concurrent API calls would require fine-grained logs of every call’s start and end time, which is often impractical. To address this, we use the average speculative step <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{K}</annotation></semantics></math> as a proxy for the true average concurrency. Let <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be the total number of planning episodes, and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the speculative steps used in episode <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>. We define</p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{K}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}{k}_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{K}=\frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M}{k}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Because each target-agent computation is relatively long-running, the <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> speculative calls in episode <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> tend to overlap in time. In practice, this means the average concurrency during episode <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is effectively <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Averaging these values over all episodes therefore yields a meaningful estimate of the sustained concurrency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main Experiment Result</h3>

<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Dynamic <math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> vs. Fixed <math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Strategies on <span id="S5.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenAGI</span> benchmark with <span id="S5.T1.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT</span> backbone</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:94pt;vertical-align:-45.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-96.1pt,26.2pt) scale(0.642124930405453,0.642124930405453) ;">
