</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Supervised vs. Reinforcement Learning for Speculative Step Prediction</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate two alternative formulations for speculative step prediction: supervised learning (SFT) and reinforcement learning (RL) with TD-based updates. While both are supported within our framework, we ultimately adopt the RL formulation for two key reasons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Theoretically, supervised learning arises as a special case of our framework. When <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the TD target reduces to the rollout length <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{t}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{t}=k</annotation></semantics></math>, thus TD-learning reduces to online supervised learning. Additionally, <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-returns provide a tunable bias–variance tradeoff between Monte Carlo and bootstrapped estimates, offering greater flexibility than pure regression. Overall, our learning framework is a more generalized framework.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Emprically, training with fully supervised targets (i.e., <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1,\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1,\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>) produced overly conservative predictors. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 6.3 Supervised vs. Reinforcement Learning for Speculative Step Prediction ‣ 6 Performance Analysis ‣ Dynamic Speculative Agent Planning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, while the Dyn. (SFT) model achieves similar or even lower token cost savings as our TD-trained variant (Dyn. (<math id="S6.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>=0.5)), it consistently underperforms in terms of latency reduction. In most cases, the performance was even slower than a fixed <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> baseline, which is the minimal non<span id="S6.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>trivial setting. Additionally, the Dyn. (SFT) model achieves lower accuracy across all settings compared to Dyn. (<math id="S6.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>=0.5) The experimental findings reveal the limitations of the supervised learning approach in this context. We attribute this to high variance in rollout-derived <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> labels and the averaging tendency of regression, which causes SFT models to underestimate <math id="S6.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in uncertain regions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S6.T9.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="S6.T9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of SFT vs. RL strategies on the <span id="S6.T9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">OpenAGI</span> benchmark.</span></figcaption>
